Changes, each for the same reason as the NEAR vault's (contracts/vault/src/lib.rs):
  1. No Entrypoint: no AccessControl, no UUPS upgrade, no proxy, no initializer, no assetConfig. Constants in its
     place: MIN_DEPOSIT (0.0001 ETH, Entrypoint's minimumDepositAmount) and MAX_RELAY_FEE_BPS (5,000, a backstop on
     the fee). No vetting fee and no withdrawFees.
  2. deposit(value, precommitment) is public on the pool; the depositor is msg.sender; usedPrecommitments moved in
     from Entrypoint.sol.
  3. The pool approves every deposit: it puts each label in its own label tree (a second LeanIMT), and a withdrawal's
     ASP root must be a root that tree has had, in place of ENTRYPOINT.latestRoot(). The circuit is unchanged: the ASP
     tree is a LeanIMT of labels either way.
  4. Every root of both trees is kept with the block time it was made at (root => unix seconds, 0 for a root the
     tree never had), in place of the 64-root ring, so a proof made against an older root still lands and any client
     reads each root's chain time with one call.
  5. No windDown and no dead: nothing can stop deposits. No ragequit, RAGEQUIT_VERIFIER or depositors record: every
     label is in the pool's own label tree, so a withdraw proof always works, and ragequit would only add a public
     exit that links a deposit to its exit.
  6. The withdrawal's processooor is the pool itself, so any relayer submits. Its data is RelayData (recipient, fee
     recipient, fee cap, deadline in ms, refund precommitment, salt), bound by the context as in the source
     (keccak256(abi.encode(withdrawal, SCOPE)) mod p). The relayer names a fee of at most the cap and at most
     MAX_RELAY_FEE_BPS of withdrawnValue; it comes out of withdrawnValue. withdrawnValue is not 0 (Entrypoint.sol).
     Whoever submits picks the fee, up to the cap, and it goes to the bound fee recipient: a proof the recipient
     submits itself binds a fee cap of 0.
  7. A reentrancy guard in transient storage on deposit and withdraw, in place of the Entrypoint's.
  8. The spend checks are one internal function, _spendChecked, which every method that spends a note calls.
  9. A precommitment at or above the field size is refused, as the NEAR vault does: Poseidon reduces it, so X + p
     would be a second note with X's nullifier.
 10. The constructor refuses to deploy unless PoseidonT3 and PoseidonT4 hold the code read on chain and the
     withdrawal verifier holds this build's code (both hashes in the source), so a pool on a chain without them
     never takes a deposit.
 11. buy (PrivacyPoolSimple, card pool-evm-buy), not in the source: a relayer releases a chunk into a swap for W in
     one transaction, and W, who holds no gas, authorizes it with an EIP-712 signature. It takes the same request and
     proof as withdraw (one ready proof serves either) and the same checks (_release: both payees, the fee against
     the cap, W's signature, _spendChecked, the backstop). W signs Buy(address adapter,bytes data,uint256 minOut,
     address token,uint256 nullifier,uint256 context,uint256 value), with the proof's nullifier, context and
     withdrawnValue. The fee goes to the fee recipient, the rest of the chunk to the adapter, and W's balance of the
     token must rise by at least minOut (never 0), else the whole call reverts. The reentrancy guard covers it.
     Adapters are separate contracts with no owner and no storage; src/adapters/UniswapV3Adapter.sol is SwapRouter02's
     exactInput with the native value (the router wraps it), the tokens out to W. A v3 pool may fill only part of an
     exact input (it stops at its price limit), and the router then keeps the rest, as ETH or as a middle token of
     the path, where anyone takes it with refundETH or sweepToken. So the adapter reverts unless the router's ETH and
     its balance of every middle token of the path are what they were before the swap: a partial fill reverts the
     whole buy and the note stays unspent (test/BuyV3.t.sol, on the router's and a v3 pool's code read from Base).
     src/adapters/UniswapV4Adapter.sol buys through Uniswap v4's PoolManager on the chain, called directly (no
     router in between): data is abi.encode(address currencyIn, PathKey[] path), currencyIn the native asset or the
     chain's WETH (the adapter wraps), each PathKey in v4-periphery's field order. Inside one unlock every hop swaps
     exactly what the hop before gave, the adapter pays in exactly what it was sent and the last hop's output goes to
     W. A v4 pool may also fill only part of an exact input, so the adapter reverts PartialFill unless every hop
     took its whole amount (nothing is left with the adapter, and the PoolManager reverts the unlock unless every
     balance the adapter has there ends at zero), and reverts TooLittleReceived when a hop gives nothing or the
     route less than minOut, before anything is paid out. A hook is part of the price: it may keep part of the input
     or output as its fee, even the part its pool would not fill, so for a hooked hop minOut is the bound; for a
     hookless hop a partial fill reverts (test/HostileV4.t.sol, from the review, with hooks that keep the unfilled
     input, credit or debit the adapter, leave a sync behind or reenter). Robinhood Chain's WETH
     (0x0Bd7D308f8E1639FAb988df18A8011f41EAcAD73) is Arbitrum's aeWETH behind an upgradeable ERC1967 proxy (its admin
     slot holds the contract 0xa3Acd31AFb851B4eB9DAD00F5204c01D924267dF), so a route from WETH there trusts that
     admin and a route from the native asset does not. Its
     interfaces are written here from v4-core's and v4-periphery's ABI (no Uniswap code is copied). Tested on forks
     of Base and Robinhood Chain with each chain's own PoolManager (test/BuyV4Fork.t.sol), with planted pools and a
     planted hook in that PoolManager for a partial fill and a hook that takes more at the buy than at the quote.
 12. withdraw and buy both refuse a request that is not exactly abi.encode(RelayData), 192 bytes: abi.decode
     accepts trailing bytes, so a request made with more bytes is never read as RelayData (this closes the open item
     pool-evm-core left).
 13. W signs every release, withdraw included, as the Solana pool does (W signs every release there): withdraw takes
     W's EIP-712 signature of Withdraw(uint256 nullifier,uint256 context,uint256 value), and buy of its Buy. Both
     carry the proof's nullifier, context and withdrawnValue, and are under the
     domain {name "Blindfill", version "1", chainId, verifyingContract: the pool}, 65 bytes with s in the lower half
     (EIP-2), and the signer must be the request's recipient, checked in _release before any spend or payment. In the
     source the relayer alone picks what a proof does; here one ready proof still serves either method, and W's
     signature at the tap picks which, so whoever sees a buy (a broken-in helper, the sequencer, anyone) cannot land
     it as a withdraw, and a signature for one proof of a note never takes another proof of it (the context and
     the value are signed, so a signature for a small chunk never takes a bigger proof of the same note and
     request). The cost: one ecrecover a withdraw (withdraw gas 485,765 to 492,420 at depth 2, PoolTest test_gas,
     forge 1.7.1 with isolate on, at 5998168 and after).

 14. SellDelegate (card pool-evm-sell), not in the source and not part of the pool: an EIP-7702 delegate for W, so W,
     which holds the bought token and no ETH, sells it and deposits the ETH back into the pool in one transaction that
     anyone sends (the helper, which pays the gas). W signs Sell(address token,uint256 amount,address target,bytes
     data,uint256 minOut,address pool,uint256 precommitment,uint256 feeCap,uint256 deadline,uint256 nonce) under the
     domain {name "Blindfill", version "1", chainId, verifyingContract: W}, 65 bytes with s in the lower half; deadline
     is in unix seconds. The delegate approves the target exactly the amount, calls it with W's calldata (a revert is
     passed on), and refuses unless the target took the whole approval, so the approval always ends at 0 and a partial
     fill reverts the sell. It unwraps the WETH the sale paid W, refuses an ETH gain under minOut (never 0), pays
     the sender the fee it names (at most feeCap) and deposits the rest with W's precommitment; ETH or WETH W already
     held is not counted. No owner, setter, selfdestruct, DELEGATECALL or CREATE; WETH is an immutable (code); the one
     stored value is W's nonce, at the ERC-7201 slot of "blindfill.sell" in W's own account, and a reentrancy guard,
     at the ERC-7201 transient slot of "blindfill.sell.guard" (as OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuardTransient keeps its
     own, so a smart account that runs this code as a module and guards itself at transient slot 0 never meets it),
     refuses a target, a token hook or a sender that calls back into W. Its own address has no key, so a call to it,
     not through W, moves nothing. test/HostileSell.t.sol holds the review's hostile cases (review of c2a8da2).
     Gas. Forge counts none of the 25,000 that EIP-7702 charges for each authorization of an empty account, and forge
     1.8.6 reports gas before refunds while 1.7.1 reports it after. So the gas limit to plan for is the 1.8.6 figure
     plus 25,000, and the L2 execution gas paid is at most the 1.7.1 figure plus 25,000; Robinhood's gasUsed adds its
     parent-chain posting gas and Base charges its L1 data fee on top, so the gas limit and the fee cap come from
     eth_estimateGas and the chain's fee reads. A sell, Uniswap v3 one hop through SwapRouter02, W's authorization in
     the same transaction, the pool's trees empty (test/SellFork.t.sol):
       chain       1.8.6    plan (+25,000)    1.7.1    L2 execution, at most (+25,000)
       Base        507,127  532,127           462,527  487,527
       Robinhood   556,763  581,763           512,163  537,163
     The deposit inserts into both trees, so each level of depth adds about 41,078 (test/Sell.t.sol
     test_gasSellMockRouterDepth0 and Depth32, a mock router: 502,050 and 1,816,560 on 1.8.6; 442,350 and 1,756,860
     on 1.7.1). The same mock sell sent as real transactions to a Prague anvil 1.8.6 (the review's run, repeated at
     this code): eth_estimateGas 524,409 with W's authorization (W empty) and 499,409 with W already delegated; the
     receipts' gasUsed 460,811 and 435,811, the 25,000 apart. On a live chain the sender sets the limit from
     eth_estimateGas; Robinhood's gasUsed also includes the cost of posting the transaction to its parent chain.

 15. SellDelegate through Uniswap v4 (card evm-sell-v4): a coin whose only pool is v4 (Clanker coins on Base, the
     hooked pools on Robinhood) has no router that pulls it, so a Sell whose target is the chain's v4 PoolManager
     (an immutable, the constructor's second argument) goes through the PoolManager directly, as v4-periphery's
     V4Router does: W unlocks it, and W's unlockCallback swaps exactly the amount along data = abi.encode(PathKey[])
     (each hop must take all it is given, else PartialFill; the route ends in the native asset or WETH, else
     InvalidRoute), settles the token with W's own transfer (sync, transfer, settle: no approval is ever given) and
     takes the whole output to W. The callback runs only when the PoolManager calls it (NotPoolManager), only once
     inside W's own sell and only with that sell's payload: the sell stores keccak256(abi.encode(token, amount, data))
     at the ERC-7201 transient slot of "blindfill.sell.v4" just before the unlock and the callback clears it
     (NotInSell otherwise: a transaction W's key sends to the PoolManager, a second callback in one unlock, or a wrong
     PoolManager pin handing back another token, amount or route, test/HostileSellV4.t.sol, review of e9f6ff9). The PoolManager reverts the unlock unless nothing is left owed (a token that
     delivers less than it moves fails with CurrencyNotSettled). minOut, the fee and the deposit are as in 14; the v3
     router path is unchanged. Gas (forge 1.7.1, one type-4 transaction with W's authorization, trees empty,
     test/SellV4Fork.t.sol): Base, Clanker coin BTC1 through its hooked WETH pool, 699,660; Robinhood, CRYBABYTRUMP
     through its hooked WETH pool, 495,617. Planted pools on Base's PoolManager code (test/SellV4.t.sol): one hop to
     ETH 491,954, to WETH 502,645, two hops 573,525 (to WETH 589,816), depth 32 1,806,385.

 16. SellDelegate's exact debit (card evm-selldelegate-exact-debit): the sell reads W's balance of the token before the
     target call (v3) or the unlock (v4) and after it, and reverts DebitNotExact unless it fell by exactly the signed
     amount, as the v3 pool checks its own balance (IIA). A token whose balanceOf shows the extra debit, taking more
     from W only on chain, where the device's eth_call probe cannot see it (an extra debit only in transferFrom, only
     toward the pool, only inside the PoolManager's unlock), and a target that gives part of the token back, both
     revert the whole sell; a balance that rose is an underflow revert (Panic 0x11); a token whose debit is the amount
     plus or minus one (share rounding) is now refused too, and W loses nothing (test/ExactDebit.t.sol,
     test/HostileSell.t.sol). A token whose balanceOf hides the extra debit passes the check: the check reads only
     what the token reports. The kit's probe stays as the early
     refusal, so the sender's gas is not spent on such a token. The cost, two balanceOf reads: the mock-router sell at
     depth 0 442,886 to 445,008 and at depth 32 1,757,396 to 1,759,518, the v4 one hop to ETH 491,954 to 494,066 and at
     depth 32 1,806,385 to 1,808,497 (forge 1.7.1, test/Sell.t.sol and test/SellV4.t.sol).
 17. SellDelegate reads WETH only for a route that can pay in it (pool review round 2, finding F3): a v3 target, or a
     v4 path whose last hop ends in WETH. A v4 sell to the native asset reads no WETH balance and unwraps nothing, so a
     WETH that reverts or misreports cannot stop it (test_v4SellToTheNativeAssetTrustsNoWeth, which puts reverting code
     at WETH's address; the two mutants that fix the route's answer to always or never are killed in test/mutants.mjs).
     The cost: the v4 one hop to ETH 494,066 to 490,040 and at depth 32 1,808,497 to 1,804,471 (two reads saved), the
     v4 one hop to WETH 504,757 to 506,926 (the path decode), the mock-router sell 445,008 to 445,273 (forge 1.7.1,
     test/Sell.t.sol and test/SellV4.t.sol).

Gas, each pool call its own transaction (isolate = true in foundry.toml), so a figure is what a sender pays: the
21,000 base, calldata and cold reads included. The kit's real proofs (test/fixtures/buy-proofs.json), fee 0.01 ETH.
The change commitment's insert hashes one level per level of the state tree, about 21,518 gas each, so a release
at depth d costs G(d) = base + 21,518 x d (depth 2: the insert at index 3; depth 32: at index 2^32 - 1, all 32
levels). Forge 1.7.1 (CI's) and 1.8.6 give the same withdraw and mock-adapter figures; through SwapRouter02 they
